# Shipped Bash hooks/scripts MUST stay LF: under core.autocrlf these get CRLF on checkout, and a
# CRLF `\r` in a shebang/command breaks the hook on Linux/macOS (and Git-Bash). Force LF regardless.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Golden wire-capture oracles must never be line-ending normalized (they contain raw protobuf
# bytes, incl. 0x0a). Without this, autocrlf would corrupt them on checkout.
*.bin binary
tests/Clavity.Ls.Tests/TestData/** binary

# Shell scripts (e.g. shipped plugin hooks) must stay LF on every platform — a CRLF *.sh
# fails on Linux/macOS with "bad interpreter". Keeps the portable hooks runnable cross-OS.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Byte-determinism for the hash-pinned registrar: identical EOL in every checkout so the build-time SHA-256
# pin (register-plugin-hash.iss) matches the packed bytes the uninstaller verifies (GetSHA256OfFile). See
# docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-19-installer-registration-convergence.md Task 5.
installer/_shared/register-plugin.ps1 text eol=crlf

# The driver-cheatsheet source and its two GENERATED literals must be LF everywhere. The pre-commit
# parity hook compares the generator's output against the INDEX blob, which git stores as LF, so a
# CRLF checkout of any of these three would mismatch on every commit. Measured 2026-08-14:
# core.autocrlf is true and `git check-attr text eol` reported `unspecified` for all three, so a fresh
# clone checked them out as CRLF. Same mechanism, same reason, as the *.sh pin above.
agy-autotrain/knowledge/driver-cheatsheet.core.md text eol=lf
clavity-classic/src/driver_cheatsheet.rs text eol=lf
clavity-dotnet/src/Clavity.Ls/DriverCheatsheet.cs text eol=lf

# The knowledge STORE must be LF everywhere. check-knowledge-store.ps1 enforces LF + pure ASCII, and
# without this pin `git check-attr text eol` reports `unspecified` for these files, so under
# core.autocrlf a fresh clone checks all 19 out as CRLF and the gate fails immediately on content it
# has never touched. MEASURED 2026-08-28 on a default `git clone` of this repository: 19 of 19 rule
# files came out CRLF and the gate reported 19 problems. CI runs on windows-latest, so that fresh
# clone IS the CI checkout - the gate would have failed on its very first run.
# Same mechanism, same reason, as the cheatsheet trio above.
agy-autotrain/knowledge/rules/*.md text eol=lf
# The bundled AiSaveDev extension ships to a browser byte for byte (install-extension.ps1 copies it):
# no line-ending conversion, so every checkout carries exactly the committed bytes.
review-relay/extension/aisavedev/** -text
# review-relay extension fixtures: the golden capture is compared BYTE FOR BYTE with what content.js
# emits (LF only). Under core.autocrlf a Windows checkout would turn it CRLF and the test would fail on
# every Windows box while passing on Linux. MEASURED 2026-09-29: an existing review-relay fixture
# checks out i/lf w/crlf without a rule like this one.
review-relay/extension/test/fixtures/** text eol=lf
